\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\looseness=-1 We test the three predictions of the symbolic mirage hypothesis (\S\ref{sec:mirage}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:independence} (Appendix) reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions at $K = 12$. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20\text{--}3.62$ as an ordinal diagnostic: higher $K_{\text{eff}}$ indicates greater vote diversity, though it should not be interpreted as a literal effective sample size (\S\ref{sec:condorcet}). Across four Mistral-7B seeds, $\kappa \in [0.41, 0.51]$ yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp, indicating seed-level constraint quality matters beyond independence. Positive $\Delta$ requires both sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ ($\leq 1.30$ never improves) and SC accuracy ($K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ with SC $= 39\%$ degrades). All four model families produce $\kappa \geq 0.41$ (Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}).


\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically align with the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. A shuffle control reveals this bias is \emph{structural}: randomizing constraint--answer associations while preserving per-trace counts yields $\text{BR}_\text{null} \approx \text{BR}_\text{obs}$ ($p > 0.7$), indicating that BR reflects the trace-count distribution rather than extraction-specific error. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, and MAX-SAT produces answers identical to count-only aggregation on 94.4\% of problems on Mistral-7B FOLIO (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), confirming that the formal verification layer is vacuous (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}): 99\% of constraints pass Z3 validation yet add no information beyond majority voting. This vacuity is $\alpha$-insensitive: agreement $\geq$99\% across $\alpha \in \{0.1, \ldots, 0.9\}$ for both high- and moderate-BR conditions (Table~\ref{tab:alpha-ablation}).

\looseness=-1 This pattern generalizes: $4.97\times$ (Mistral), $3.85\times$ (Qwen-14B), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA), all $> 1.0$ with 95\% bootstrap CIs (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Cross-model extraction confirms SCB universality across nine models from 7B to frontier (BR $1.66$--$4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}). BR $> 1$ further holds across all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix on FOLIO and ProofWriter (range $1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero cross-answer overlap), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Symbolic confirmation bias across nine models on FOLIO ($n = 204$). BR: mean per-problem bias ratio. $n_\text{err}$: number of incorrect instances on which BR is computed. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). Ext.: $^a$self-extraction; $^b$cross-model (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). $^*$Interim ($n = 95/204$). All models exhibit $\text{BR} > 1$; BR estimates for high-accuracy models ($n_\text{err} < 40$) have wider confidence intervals; Gemini ($n_\text{err} \approx 12$) should be interpreted with particular caution. Process-level similarity ($\kappa_{\text{proc}}$) ranges from $0.25$ (R1-Distill) to $0.79$ (gpt-4o/4.1/Gemini); see Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}.}
\label{tab:frontier-scb}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Ext. & SC (\%) & $n_\text{err}$ & BR & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & $^a$ & 55.88 & 90 & 4.97 & 0.00 \\
LLaMA-8B & $^a$ & \textrm{---} & \textrm{---} & 2.88 & \textrm{---} \\
Qwen-14B & $^a$ & 75.98 & 49 & 3.85 & $-$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
R1-Distill-8B & $^b$ & 74.51 & 52 & 2.92 & $+$2.45 \\
gpt-4o & $^a$ & 73.91 & 53 & 2.94 & $-$1.80 \\
gpt-4.1 & $^b$ & 82.84 & 35 & 2.70 & $-$1.47 \\
gpt-5.5 & $^b$ & 84.31 & 32 & 3.48 & $+$0.49 \\
o3 & $^b$ & 82.65 & 35 & 1.66 & 0.00 \\
Gemini-2.5-Pro$^*$ & $^b$ & 87.37 & 12 & 2.79 & $-$1.10 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Two controls rule out answer leakage. BR is uniform across early, mid, and late reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$); high-consensus errors amplify to $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$ versus $2.7\times$ for low-consensus ($p < 0.001$). Removing the answer sentence from traces before extraction yields no BR decrease (Wilcoxon $p \geq 0.12$) despite significant accuracy loss (McNemar $p < 0.013$). SCB reflects a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer contamination.

\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

\looseness=-1 We quantify reasoning-process overlap via ProcessSim, the mean pairwise cosine similarity of TF-IDF trace vectors (Eq.~\ref{eq:process-sim}, Appendix~\ref{app:process-gradient}). ProcessSim captures lexical overlap; deeper structure measures may reveal additional patterns, so we treat it as supporting rather than primary evidence. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean ProcessSim $= 0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.46$: traces share reasoning processes even when answers diverge. The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}), from $0.31$ (LLaMA) to $0.02$ (Qwen-14B). Under cross-model extraction, SICA gain decreases monotonically with ProcessSim ($\rho = -1.0$, $p = 0.008$; Table~\ref{tab:process-gradient}), modulated by self-bias severity.


CCR analysis confirms the same pattern: cross-architecture NLI yields $\text{CCR} > 0$ while same-model SICA yields $\text{CCR} < 0$, with CCR correlating with combo gain ($\rho = 0.84$, $p = 0.014$; Appendix~\ref{app:cil}).

\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:diagnostics}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 All 27 training-free remediation strategies spanning four categories produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). Four representative failure modes illuminate the independence-accuracy trade-off. \emph{Debate} ($\kappa = 0.15$, $\Delta = -18.6$\,pp) successfully breaks trace correlation but collapses accuracy---same-model diversity and quality are at odds. \emph{Cross-model extraction} (using a different LLM as extractor) decreases $\kappa$ slightly but $\Delta \approx 0$, because same-family models share systematic biases (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). \emph{Contrastive prompting} produces imbalanced output (94 pro vs.\ 65 con constraints), indicating confirmation bias transfers to constraint generation. The \emph{oracle ceiling} ($+29.90$\,pp with gold constraints) proves the mechanism is sound; self-extraction captures only ${\sim}$12\%, localizing the bottleneck to extraction quality. All non-debate strategies cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, a same-model ceiling across three families. No individual strategy achieves significance after correction (Appendix~\ref{app:intervention-individual}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_interventions_compact.pdf}
\caption{Twenty-three of 27 training-free strategies across four categories (four additional strategies tested but omitted for clarity). All produce neutral-to-negative $\Delta$ vs.\ SC. Grey band: $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Prospective held-out validation.}
\looseness=-1 Decision boundaries ($C_0 < 5\%$, $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.3$, SC $< 40\%$) were derived from Mistral-7B and Qwen2.5-14B conditions. We evaluate prospectively on held-out LLaMA-3.1-8B, a different model family not used in boundary derivation: on FOLIO (SC $= 66.18\%$, $C_0 = 61.8\%$, $K_\text{eff} = 2.17$), the framework predicts Type~A confirmation bias ($\Delta \leq 0$); the observed $\Delta = -4.41$\,pp confirms the prediction.

\paragraph{Symbolic Mirage Hypothesis: verification.}
\looseness=-1 The observations and prediction of \S\ref{sec:mirage} are confirmed. Observations O1--O2, initially discovered on Mistral-7B, generalize: BR $> 1$ across three self-extraction models, six cross-model pairs, and all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}); 27 training-free strategies fail to reduce BR below 1.0 (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). Prediction P1 is validated: cross-architecture NLI escapes the mirage for generators up to SC $\approx 70\%$ (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Self-Consistency and extensions.}
SC selects the most frequent answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}. Extensions include universal SC for free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting with step-aware verification \citep{li2023diverse}, program-based vote filtering \citep{toh2024notallvotes}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, kim2026reasc}. \citet{loo2025reevaluating} show that SC gains plateau early; \citet{byerly2026selfconsistency} further analyze SC's limitations in mathematical reasoning. Our Condorcet analysis explains this: $\kappa = 0.21$--$0.82$ compresses $K_{\text{eff}}$ to 1.20--3.62 for $K = 12$ (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}).

\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
We distinguish three paradigms. \emph{Specification-generation} (Logic-LM \citep{pan2023logiclm}, LINC \citep{olausson2023linc}, SatLM \citep{ye2024satlm}) translates problem descriptions into formal specifications before any answer is generated; LINC documents ${\sim}$40\% translation errors even at this level. \emph{Program-generation} \citep{gao2023pal,chen2023program,lyu2023faithful} produces executable code from problem statements. Both paradigms formalize \emph{before} answer generation. \emph{Trace-extraction} (this work) operates post-hoc on traces that already contain candidates, inheriting confirmation bias (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Recent extensions \citep{ryu2025clover, xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, singh2026verge, luo2025logicagent, miya2025eidoku} introduce capabilities beyond the reasoning model. \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show formalization degrades with complexity; \citet{chan2026position} argue logical soundness is unreliable for neurosymbolic verification. Our oracle controls confirm the mechanism is sound ($+29.90$/$+13.73$\,pp FOLIO, $+17.17$\,pp ProofWriter); the failure localizes to self-extraction (Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}).

\paragraph{Test-time compute and verification.}
Scaling test-time computation through additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling} or structured search \citep{yao2023tree} faces the same correlation challenge: same-model attempts collapse into correlated solutions. Reasoning models (o1, DeepSeek-R1 \citep{guo2025deepseekr1}, QwQ \citep{qwen2024qwq}) extend thinking time but remain subject to correlated same-model voting: QwQ-32B produces $\kappa = 0.91$ and $C_0 = 2.6\%$, suggesting that longer reasoning chains intensify homogenization rather than alleviating it. Trained verifiers offer an alternative \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k, lightman2024prm, wang2024mathshepherd}; multi-agent debate refines answers through discussion \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}. Self-verification \citep{weng2023selfverification}, chain-of-verification \citep{dhuliawala2023chainofverification}, self-refinement \citep{madaan2023selfrefine}, tool-augmented critique \citep{gou2024critic}, and formal CoT verification \citep{zhu2025vericot} report gains, but none systematically tests self-extracted constraints under formal solvers. Recent self-verification methods in test-time scaling \citep{chen2025sets} show positive results using trained verification signals, contrasting with our negative findings for untrained symbolic extraction; multi-agent verification approaches \citep{lifshitz2025mav} align with our cross-architecture evidence that structurally independent verifiers can escape SCB (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}). We find the bottleneck shifts from the verifier to the extractor: self-extracted constraints inherit the model's bias regardless of verification formalism (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Our diagnostics (BR, $K_\text{eff}$, process-$\kappa$) predict when trace-aggregation will fail.

\paragraph{CJT and independence.}
CJT \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees convergence under independent voting; \citet{ladha1992correlated} show correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size. Our $K_\text{eff}$ adapts Fleiss' $\kappa$ from ensemble diversity theory \citep{kuncheva2003measures, brown2005diversity}. \citet{kim2025correlated} demonstrate correlated errors in LLM outputs; \citet{kuai2026independent} propose BEI and CIG for cross-model independence auditing, complementary to our within-model process-$\kappa$. SCB is distinct from CoT unfaithfulness \citep{turpin2024language} and CoT-level confirmation bias \citep{wan2025unveiling, jhaveri2026failing}: the latter concern whether reasoning reflects actual computation, while SCB concerns whether \emph{self-extracted formal constraints} mirror the model's answer distribution.

\paragraph{Positioning.}
\looseness=-1 \citet{huang2024selfcorrect} show LLMs cannot self-correct without external feedback---a general observation about natural-language self-correction. We provide a specific, quantified diagnosis in the formal verification setting: provably sound Z3 verification adds no information beyond majority voting under self-extraction (BR $1.66$--$4.97\times$ across nine models), a \emph{symbolic mirage} not addressed by prior frameworks. Neuro-symbolic methods with positive results \citep{ye2024satlm, xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover} succeed because they use specification-generation, trained extractors, or curated knowledge; our finding explains \emph{why} self-extraction fails---constraints inherit the model's distributional prior. Prior formalization studies note high error rates \citep{olausson2023linc, amonkar2025realitycheck} but treat them as quality problems; we show the errors are systematically biased toward the model's answer (shuffle control: $\text{BR}_\text{null} \approx \text{BR}_\text{obs}$, $p > 0.7$), a distributional bias distinct from random translation errors.

\enlargethispage{14\baselineskip}
\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1
The three diagnostics ($K_\text{eff}$, BR, $C_0$) extend beyond SICA to any trace-aggregation method, correctly classifying all 60 tested conditions. Decision boundaries generalize prospectively to a held-out model family (\S\ref{sec:diagnostics}; Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}), enabling practitioners to predict viability from a pilot run ($K{=}12$) at zero cost.

\looseness=-1
SCB applies to any method extracting structured representations---self-verification \citep{huang2024selfcorrect}, self-refinement \citep{madaan2023selfrefine}, chain-of-verification \citep{dhuliawala2023chainofverification} all face the same distributional prior. Escaping requires (i)~\emph{structural independence}---the verifier must not share architecture or training data with the generator; (ii)~\emph{capability ordering}---combo gain decreases monotonically with generator SC; zero-shot NLI benefits only the weakest generator, while domain-adapted NLI extends gains to generators with SC up to 72\%; (iii)~\emph{task-verifier alignment} (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}). For test-time scaling \citep{chen2025sets, lifshitz2025mav}, with $K_\text{eff} \leq 3.6$ for $K{=}12$, same-model traces scale redundancy, not information---effective scaling requires structurally independent verifiers or trained signals escaping the model's prior.

